2. Add Two Numbers in linkedlist

Explanation:

first create a dummy node and initiate a new result linkedlist.
second, make a pointer named temp for the result linkeldist named dummy.
next set carry =0
third, the while loop is such that l1!=null or l2!=null or carry!=0 becuase if we reach the end of both the linkedlist, 
we might still have carry having some value and we may have to put it, so run the loop until carry is not null.
fourth, in the while loop set sum=0, and check if every node in l1 and l2 is not null, then add the node's value to the sum and got to the next node in the linkedlist.

fifth, after l1 and l2, add the carry to the sum.
sixth,calculation carry as sum/10 and remainder as sum%10.
Create a new node and put the remainder value in it and direct it such that temp.next=new node
then move temp=temp.next;

finally return dummy.next;


Code:
/**
 * Definition for singly-linked list.
 * public class ListNode {
 *     int val;
 *     ListNode next;
 *     ListNode() {}
 *     ListNode(int val) { this.val = val; }
 *     ListNode(int val, ListNode next) { this.val = val; this.next = next; }
 * }
 */
class Solution {
    public ListNode addTwoNumbers(ListNode l1, ListNode l2) {
        ListNode dummy = new ListNode();
        ListNode temp = dummy;

        int carry=0;
        while(l1!=null|| l2!=null || carry!=0){
            int sum=0;

            if(l1!=null){
                sum+=l1.val;
                l1=l1.next;
            }
            if(l2!=null){
                sum+=l2.val;
                l2=l2.next;
            }
            sum+=carry;
            carry=sum/10;
            int remainder =  sum%10;
            ListNode node = new ListNode(remainder);
            temp.next=node;
            temp=temp.next;
        }

        return dummy.next;
    }
}
